.TH CVB 1 "November 2026" "CVB 0.7.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cvb - terminal-to-LLM exchange utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cvb
.br
.B cvb pull
.RI [ file:PATH ]...
.RI [ cascade:N ]
.I search-term...
.br
.B cvb pull
.BI file: PATH
.RB [ head:\fIN\fR | tail:\fIN\fR ]
.br
.B cvb pull
.BI file: PATH
.BI lines: SPEC
.RB [ head:\fIN\fR | tail:\fIN\fR ]
.br
.B cvb pull
.BI file: PATH
.BI lines: SPEC
.I search-term...
.br
.B cvb pull
.BI callers: NAME
.RI [ file:PATH ]
.br
.B cvb context
.br
.B cvb
.I path
.br
.B cvb
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B CVB
(Capture (-> View (-> Bridge) is a terminal utility for reliable exchange between a human, the terminal, the clipboard, and an LLM.

CVB can capture completed commands and their terminal output in a resident Bash session, capture the currently visible terminal screen with a configurable hotkey, bridge files to the clipboard, recursively inventory directories as TSV, and execute external commands.

CVB is not a shell replacement, terminal logger, terminal database, or AI agent.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (no arguments)
Start a resident CVB Bash session. The Bash wrapper must have been sourced (see RESIDENT CAPTURE).

Commands entered in the session are captured when they complete. Their visible terminal output is copied to the Wayland clipboard.

The active session is indicated by a red dot in the shell prompt. The session ends with the shell's own
.BR exit .
.TP
.B pull \fR[\fBfile:\fIPATH\fR]... [\fBcascade:\fIN\fR] \fIsearch-term...\fR
Search source files: Git-tracked ones and untracked ones that .gitignore does not hide. Rust is parsed syntactically by item, Bash by function, Python (.py, .pyi) by function and class, Zig (.zig) by function, test and type, C (.c, .h) and C++ (.cc, .cpp, .cxx, .hh, .hpp, .hxx) by function and type, JavaScript (.js, .mjs, .cjs, .jsx) by function and class, TypeScript (.ts, .mts, .cts, .tsx) by function, class and type, Go (.go) by function and type, Java (.java) by method and type, Lua (.lua) by function, and Markdown by heading. Other files are searched by line. Callers and cascade work on every language listed here except Markdown.

Matching is case-sensitive. Bare terms are alternatives (OR),
.BI + term
is required and
.BI - term
excludes units that contain it (at least one positive term is needed); a quoted argument is an exact phrase.
.BI file: PATH
restricts the search to the named files (any number; repeats are ignored and their order does not matter; a path that matches no file fails the whole command) or, alone, prints the whole file.
.BI head: N
and
.BI tail: N
(N at least 1) print the first or last N lines of a whole file, or trim
.B lines:
output; they cannot be combined with search terms, callers:, cascade: or each other.
.BI lines: SPEC
(N or START-END, several allowed, exactly one file:) prints those lines of the file. With search terms it instead restricts the search: a hit must lie in one of the ranges, and the unit around it is returned whole, so it may extend beyond the range. + and - terms are still checked over the whole unit.
.BI callers: NAME
lists syntactic call sites in Rust, Bash, Python, Zig, C, C++, JavaScript, TypeScript, Go, Java and Lua (candidates, not proof; a call through a receiver, such as a.f(), is reported as a method-call candidate).
.BI cascade: N
(0 to 5) adds the units that the matches depend on: for Rust by calls and signature types, for the other languages by calls matched by name.
.TP
.B context
Print the launch context text (the manual), byte-identical to the launch clipboard.
.TP
.B --help, -h
Display command-line help.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Display the CVB version.
.TP
.I path
If the argument names an existing filesystem path, bridge it according to its type.

Regular files are copied to the clipboard unchanged. Directories are recursively represented as TSV.
.TP
.I command
If the first argument does not name an existing filesystem path, execute it as an external command with the remaining arguments.

Common terminal pagers are disabled while executing commands so their output can be captured directly.
.SH RESIDENT CAPTURE
The resident mode is normally started through the Bash wrapper supplied with CVB.

Source the wrapper into the parent Bash shell, then run
.B cvb
with no arguments. The session ends with
.BR exit .
.PP
The wrapper creates a temporary shell-state file and starts a child Bash session. Useful parent-shell state is transferred to the child, including aliases, functions, shell options, shopt settings, and inherited environment variables.
.PP
CVB owns the capture-related prompt and shell hooks while the session is active. The parent shell is restored when the child session exits. Commands entered during the session are added to the parent shell's history.
.SH SCREEN CAPTURE
While a resident CVB session is running, CVB maintains a representation of the terminal's currently visible screen.
.PP
The default screen-capture binding is:
.PP
.EX
Ctrl+Shift+Space
.EE
.PP
Pressing the binding copies the current visible terminal screen to the Wayland clipboard.
.PP
Screen capture works with both ordinary shell sessions and full-screen interactive terminal applications such as
.B htop
and
.BR micro .
The captured text represents the current visible terminal screen rather than command history or terminal scrollback.
.PP
The screen-capture binding is configured through:
.PP
.EX
~/.config/cvb/keybind
.EE
.PP
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is instead located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/keybind .
If the file does not exist, CVB creates it with the default binding.
.PP
Each non-empty, non-comment line defines one binding. Multiple bindings are allowed.
.PP
Supported modifiers are
.BR Ctrl ,
.BR Shift ,
.BR Alt ,
and
.BR Super .
The key is separated from modifiers with
.BR + .
.PP
Named special keys include
.BR Enter ,
.BR Escape ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR Space ,
.BR Backspace ,
.BR Delete ,
.BR Insert ,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR PageUp ,
.BR PageDown ,
.BR Left ,
.BR Right ,
.BR Up ,
.BR Down ,
and
.BR F1
through
.BR F24 .
.PP
Named punctuation keys include
.BR Plus ,
.BR Minus ,
.BR Comma ,
.BR Period ,
.BR Slash ,
.BR Backslash ,
.BR Semicolon ,
.BR Apostrophe ,
.BR LeftBracket ,
.BR RightBracket ,
and
.BR Backtick .
.PP
The configuration is watched while CVB is running. Changes take effect without restarting the resident session.
.PP
CVB observes configured key combinations but does not consume the underlying keyboard events. The terminal or application may therefore also receive the key combination.
.SH FILE BRIDGING
An existing filesystem path takes precedence over an external command.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.EX
cvb /path/to/file.txt
.EE
.PP
copies the complete contents of the file to the Wayland clipboard.
.SH DIRECTORY BRIDGING
Directories are recursively represented as tab-separated values.
.PP
The output has the following form:
.PP
.EX
path	type	size
one.txt	file	4
subdir	dir	-
subdir/two.txt	file	4
.EE
.PP
Paths are relative to the selected directory. Regular files report their byte size and directories use
.BR - .
Symlinks and other filesystem object types are skipped.
.SH IGNORE LIST
CVB creates
.I ~/.config/cvb/ignore
on first directory inventory. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is instead located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/ignore .
.PP
The file contains one directory name per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored. A directory name is excluded wherever it occurs in the directory tree.
.PP
The initial file excludes common version-control directories:
.PP
.EX
\&.git
\&.hg
\&.svn
\&.bzr
\&.jj
.EE
.PP
The file is the complete source of truth: remove an entry to include that directory, or add entries such as
.B target
or
.B node_modules
to exclude them.
.PP
CVB does not interpret glob patterns, regular expressions, or
.BR .gitignore
syntax.
.SH CLIPBOARD
CVB uses
.B wl-copy
for Wayland clipboard integration.
.PP
When a captured command produces no visible terminal output, CVB copies a message
naming the command and stating whether it succeeded or failed, including its exit status.
A command line that ran several commands is reported as a command block with the commands observed.
Visible terminal output is preserved and introduced by the label
.BR "Terminal output:" .
.PP
Commands pasted at the shell prompt of a resident session (a bracketed paste) form a command block.
CVB removes the paste markers and lets the shell decide where each command ends: the primary prompt and the continuation prompt each print a marker, so every line the shell reads is accounted for. A command that spans lines (a compound command, a heredoc, a quoted string, a backslash continuation) is one command; blank lines and comments are not commands. When the last line has been read it copies one report that starts with
.BR "Command block results:" .
For each command the report gives
.BR "Command i/N:" ,
the command's own output and
.B Exit status: 0 (PASS)
or
.BR "Exit status: N (FAIL)" ,
and it ends with
.BR "Command block: P passed, F failed, I incomplete." .
A command that did not finish before the shell ended or was interrupted, or that was still open when the paste ended, is marked
.BR "Exit status: INCOMPLETE" ;
the report is copied at that point and lines never reached are listed as not started.
Input the shell refuses to run, such as a syntax error, is reported as failed.
The lines run in order and a failing line does not stop the next one.
A command that reads standard input consumes the following lines of the paste, so the block does not finish.
Pastes that contain control characters other than tab and line endings, are not valid UTF-8, exceed 3 MiB or start while a program is running reach the shell unchanged.
.PP
Commands listed in the skip list are an exception: when they produce no visible output the clipboard is left unchanged.
Output, including error messages, is captured as usual.
.PP
Screen captures copy the textual contents of the currently visible terminal screen.
.SH SKIP LIST
CVB creates
.I ~/.config/cvb/skip
when a resident session starts. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is instead located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/skip .
.PP
The file contains one command name per line. Blank lines and text after
.B #
are ignored. The initial file lists
.BR cd .
.PP
A command line is skipped only when every top-level command in it is listed, matched by its first word, and the line produced no visible output.
Commands inside functions and compound commands such as
.B if
are not matched.
The file is read on every command, so changes apply immediately. Screen captures are not affected.
.SH PATCH PASTE
Inside a resident session, a patch in
.B apply_patch
format pasted at the shell prompt is applied automatically. The paste must be exactly one patch, from a line
.B *** Begin Patch
to a line
.BR "*** End Patch" ,
sent as a bracketed paste by the terminal.
.PP
A unified diff, as printed by
.BR "git diff" ,
is accepted the same way. It must start with a line
.B diff --git
or with
.B --- a/\&...
followed by a
.B +++
line, and use the default
.B a/
and
.B b/
path prefixes. It is applied with
.B git apply
(found in
.BR PATH )
on the same scratch copy and committed in the same transaction. Modified, new (with the stated file mode), deleted and renamed files are supported. Binary patches, copies, symbolic links, submodules, mode changes and merge diffs are refused with a reason. The line counts in the hunk headers are not trusted: every hunk is recounted from its lines, headers that were wrong are corrected, and the report says so. A diff that ends right after a hunk header is refused.
.PP
CVB runs the
.B apply_patch
program found in
.B PATH
on a scratch copy of the files the patch names, then commits the result as one transaction, so a patch is applied completely or not at all. The root is the current directory of the shell. Supported are
.BR "Update File" ,
.BR "Add File" ,
.B "Delete File"
and
.BR "Move to" .
An
.B Add File
whose path exists is refused. Files must be UTF-8 text. Paths must be relative, without
.B ..
or a
.B .git
component, and must not pass through a symlink.
.PP
The result is printed, copied to the clipboard and the prompt is drawn again. It begins with
.B CVB patch APPLIED files=N\c
,
.B CVB patch FAILED (apply_patch exit N)
followed by the output of
.B apply_patch
and
.BR "No files were modified." ,
or
.B CVB patch REJECTED: reason\c
\&. Nothing is added to the shell history.
.PP
When a patch fails because its changes are already present in the files (for example, it was pasted twice), the output of
.B apply_patch
is followed by a line starting with
.B Note:
that names the files. The note only explains the failure; nothing is modified.
.PP
A paste is examined only while the shell waits for a command. A paste that is not a single patch is handled as a command block (see CLIPBOARD) or, when it cannot be, passed to the shell unchanged; a patch without its end line is refused.
.PP
The environment variable
.B CVB_PATCH_PASTE
set to
.BR off ,
.BR 0 ,
.B false
or
.B no
disables the feature.
.PP
With
.B CVB_PATCH_REVIEW
set to
.BR on ,
.BR 1 ,
.B true
or
.B yes
a recognized patch is shown (its text, a summary of the files, and any correction CVB made) instead of applied, and nothing is copied. Pressing
.B Ctrl+B
twice applies it; any other key cancels it and is passed on to the shell. A patch pasted while another waits replaces it.
.PP
When a patch fails because hunks do not match the files, CVB reports the failure (nothing is modified) and offers up to five candidate locations for each failing hunk. Places where only context lines differ come first; places where a line to be removed differs or is missing are offered only when there is nothing better, and the screen says how many were left out. Use the left and right arrow keys (or up and down) to browse candidates and
.B Enter
to select the highlighted candidate. Number keys select a candidate directly.
.PP
CVB copies a review bundle to the clipboard: for each failing hunk, the patch as supplied and every candidate with its differences, its score, the enclosing scope and the surrounding file lines. The user may paste this bundle into an assistant for help evaluating which location is semantically correct. Candidate scores are suggestions, not proof; CVB does not silently decide ambiguous relocations.
.PP
After the last choice the corrected patch is shown. A warning appears when a line to be removed differs from the one the patch named (the real file line would be removed), and another when a line to be removed is not in the file (that removal is dropped and nothing is deleted for it). Pressing
.B s
skips (which cancels the whole patch).
.B q ,
.B Esc
or an unknown key cancels the picker; unrecognized input is passed on to the shell. Pressing
.B Ctrl+B
twice applies the corrected patch. If a file the patch changes has different contents from when the candidates were shown (compared by content; touching a file does not count), CVB changes nothing, reports
.BR "CVB patch REJECTED: FILE changed since the candidates were shown" ,
and closes the picker; the patch must be pasted again. There is no overall inactivity timeout; CVB briefly waits 30 milliseconds to distinguish a split Escape sequence from a standalone Escape.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
Existing filesystem paths take precedence over external commands.
.PP
For example, if
.B Cargo.toml
exists in the current directory,
.B cvb Cargo.toml
bridges the file rather than attempting to execute a command named
.BR Cargo.toml .
.PP
If the path does not exist, the arguments are treated as an external command.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/cvb
The CVB executable when installed system-wide.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cvb/cvb.bash
Bash wrapper providing the
.B cvb
command that starts a session.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cvb/
Temporary CVB shell-state and Bash initialization files.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cvb/keybind
Screen-capture key bindings. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/keybind .
.TP
.I ~/.config/cvb/ignore
Directory names excluded from directory inventories. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/ignore .
.TP
.I ~/.config/cvb/skip
Commands whose output is not captured when they print nothing. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/skip .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/cvb/surgery/
Journals and scratch copies of patch transactions (default
.IR ~/.local/state/cvb/surgery/ ).
.SH REQUIREMENTS
CVB requires:
.IP \(bu 2
Linux
.IP \(bu 2
Bash
.IP \(bu 2
Wayland
.IP \(bu 2
wl-clipboard
.IP \(bu 2
git (for
.B cvb pull
and pasted unified diffs)
.IP \(bu 2
apply_patch in
.B PATH
(for pasted
.B apply_patch
patches)
.IP \(bu 2
the libinput, libudev and libcanberra libraries
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, or the manual for
.B cvb context
could not be read.
.TP
.B non-zero
General error or external command returned non-zero status.
